# Veil Iris Lights Compat

## Version 1.6.1 - Added line lights and capsule lenses, full-area light beams

### Added

- Long spot-light lenses: AreaLightShapeRegistry.set(SpotLightData, focus, lensRadius, lensHalfLength) stretches the round lens into a capsule along the light's local x axis (e.g. fluorescent tubes); surfaces and light beams are lit from the whole length
- Area lights with an angle of 360 degrees (2 pi) shine evenly in every direction from their rectangle, so a line light needs one light instead of two back-to-back 180 degree lights

### Changed

- Area lights with a 180 degree angle now fade out towards their back like in Veil's own renderer (smooth falloff over the angle to the normal) instead of shining fully in every direction; two of them back to back form an even line light (used by L?YM! for rows of light tubes)
- Light beams of area lights now come from their whole rectangle (before: from its center point); round spot lenses still shrink to their center for beams (see the flashlight snow fix), capsule lenses to their center line

## Version 1.6.0 - Added spot lights and light beams, fixed outlines and a crash

### Added

- Veil spot lights (SpotLightData) are now rendered with Iris shaderpacks and in the fallback pass, including voxel occlusion. They share the area-light budget
- Optional spot-light shaping for addons: AreaLightShapeRegistry.set(SpotLightData, focus, lensRadius) adds beam focus and a lens radius; the soft edge comes from Veil's own spot angle
- Light beams: Veil's in-scattering strength (point, area and spot lights) is now rendered as visible light in the air, with voxel shadows and dithered raymarching. New "Light beams" on/off toggle and "Light beam quality" option (Low/Medium/High = 8/16/32 steps, default on and Medium); costs nothing when no light has in-scattering
- Light extras API for addons: AreaLightShapeRegistry.setExtras(LightData, skipFirstPerson, chromaticFringe) lets a light skip the first-person hand/held item (e.g. flashlights) and adds a chromatic fringe at the cone edge

### Changed

- The voxel shadow option is now a master switch: when enabled, every Veil light casts voxel shadows regardless of its own occlusion flag (light mods often leave it off, so the option appeared to do nothing)
- Spot-light lenses are round: the lens radius used to clamp to a square whose corners followed the light's roll, so beams looked square and rotated with the view direction
- Removed the "Detailed normals" option: since the depth-edge fixes it made no visible difference. Normals now always use the precise neighbour sampling within 64 blocks; its slot in the settings screen holds the new light beam toggle
- Light beams are raymarched in a separate half-resolution pass (same shader, beam output only) and upsampled bilinearly in the composite: about a quarter of the beam cost, which matters for wide, long-range lights (e.g. a 64 block hanging lamp). The full-resolution lighting pass no longer computes beams
- First-person hand detection now compares against Iris' pre-hand depth buffer (pixel exact) instead of a fixed screen region; lights other than skipFirstPerson lights now light the hand

### Fixed

- Overlapping colored lights mix properly (red + blue = magenta etc.): each light is now compressed on its own before accumulation instead of compressing only the sum, which let the closer light dominate the hue completely and split overlaps along a hard edge. Single lights look unchanged
- Fixed black pixels around foliage, leaves and grass with shaderpacks: neighbouring sky pixels reconstructed to infinite positions and turned the surface normal into NaN
- Fixed dashed dark outlines along depth edges (grass blades, flowers, leaves in front of the ground), mainly with detailed normals off: the screen-space derivatives paired edge pixels with a neighbour on another surface, tilting the normal away from the light. Depth edges are now detected relative to the pixel footprint and use the closer neighbour (or a camera-facing normal for one-pixel-wide blades)
- Large FPS drops with active Veil lights:
  - Light beams only raymarch where the view ray passes through a light's range instead of along the whole ray for every light
  - Voxel shadows inside light beams only run on "High" beam quality (they cost steps x lights x shadow ray per pixel)
  - Surface lighting skips the shadow ray for light contributions below ~0.2 % (invisible fringes of a light's range)
- Remaining dark outlines around small depth steps (flower petals on grass, blade bottoms), which were too short for the edge threshold: within 64 blocks normals now always use the closer of both neighbours
- Light beams swimming around while walking, also for static spotlights: the beam rays started at the camera position without view bobbing, while the scene is rendered with it. Rays now start at the real eye position taken from the projection
- White "snow" (a dithered pattern of bright dots, most visible in the sky) with a flashlight: light beams sampled spot lights with their lens as an area, so a beam sample just in front of a lens at the eye counted as fully inside the cone regardless of the view direction and covered a whole raymarch step. Beams now use the lens center as a point emitter; surface lighting keeps the area lens
- Visible grid / snow pattern inside and around light beams: the beam dither, upscaled from the half-resolution beam pass, formed a regular pattern (most visible on Low quality). The composite now blurs the beams slightly (3x3 bilinear taps), which removes the pattern; beams are soft fog, so no detail is lost
- Crash (access violation in the graphics driver) when enabling voxel shadows while a shaderpack is active: Veil creates the voxel grid texture on first use with a client-memory upload and does not reset the pixel unpack state, which Iris' pipeline can leave with a bound unpack buffer or skip values. VILC now resets the unpack state around the voxel grid setup and restores it afterwards

## Version 1.5.1 - Veil 4.5.2, stronger colors, fixed magenta checkerboard

### Changed

- Updated to Veil 4.5.2 (minimum required version)
- Shaderpack detection now uses the official Iris API
- License metadata set to the YesMenn Custom License 1.0
- Built against Sodium 0.8.13 (minimum stays 0.8.12-beta.1); Iris 1.8.14-beta.1 is still the latest for 1.21.1
- Addons can register a combined config screen (ConfigScreenBridge.registerCombinedScreen); the mod list entry and config key then open it
- Removed the now unused resolved-light texture (one less full-screen RGBA16F texture)
- Removed the unused bundled glsl-transformer library
- Corrected mod descriptions: VILC renders Veil point and area lights and no longer runs Veil shader programs through Iris

### Fixed

- Fixed a thin dark ring around the player at 48 blocks: surface-normal derivatives were evaluated in non-uniform control flow at the detailed-normals range boundary
- Saturated light colors are much stronger with shaderpacks: the shaderpack pass now composites from a scene copy like the vanilla pass instead of GL blending into the 8-bit target, which clamped the light term and capped colored light at 2x the scene color
- Fixed the full-screen magenta checkerboard and magenta first-person hand with Iris installed: Veil's own deferred light pass is skipped while Veil keeps its albedo buffer disabled under Iris; VILC renders the lights instead
- Guarded voxel-occlusion light checks against Veil light handles without light data

## Version 1.4.14 - Fixed lost green light and unlit spots on first-person items

### Fixed

- Replaced per-channel destination-color light blending with screen-style blending so green light remains visible on red or low-green first-person item textures
- Removed the screen-space first-person filter that could leave rectangular unlit areas around the hand and held item

## Version 1.4.13 - Reworked the hand light filter against magenta tint

### Fixed

- Reworked the shaderpack first-person hand and held-item light filter to avoid magenta-only white light tint when green contribution is lost on the item layer

## Version 1.4.12 - Fixed magenta tint from white lights on the hand

### Fixed

- Tightened the shaderpack first-person hand and held-item light filter so white lights no longer leave magenta-only tint from missing green contribution

## Version 1.4.11 - Restored texture detail, reduced stripes with shaderpacks

### Changed

- Restored texture-aware shaderpack light blending so bright lights color existing surfaces instead of covering block texture detail
- Smoothed shaderpack light falloff and reduced distant surface-normal artifacts that could create hard stripe patterns

### Fixed

- Reduced first-person hand and held-item tinting from nearby shaderpack lights without reintroducing the broad near-camera unlit bubble

## Version 1.4.10 - Fixed backwards area lights and magenta tint near the camera

### Fixed

- Fixed Veil/Nox area lights rendering in the opposite direction in the Iris shaderpack pass
- Reduced magenta first-person and screen overlay tinting from nearby visible area-light glow surfaces

## Version 1.4.9 - Fixed over-tinted hands and held items near white lights

### Fixed

- Fixed first-person hands and held items being over-tinted by nearby white VILC lights
- The shaderpack light pass now skips extremely near-camera fragments so world lighting does not affect first-person item rendering

## Version 1.4.6 - Restored fallback pass, fixed Colored Lights disappearing

### Changed

- Restored the non-shaderpack light fallback pass for setups where Veil lights need VILC to appear on the main world target
- The non-shaderpack fallback pass now reads YesMenn Colored Light render settings when YesMenn is installed

### Fixed

- Fixed Colored Lights disappearing without shaderpacks after separating the YesMenn and VILC config panels
- Fixed VILC shader settings affecting normal non-shaderpack world lighting

## Version 1.4.5 - Limited VILC color settings to shaderpack rendering

### Changed

- VILC no longer renders its normal main-world light pass while shaderpacks are disabled
- VILC color tuning is now limited to shaderpack rendering and Vista live-feed rendering

### Fixed

- Fixed VILC color settings also affecting normal non-shaderpack Colored Lights when YesMenn provides its own Veil tuning

Veil Iris Lights Compat bridges Veil rendering and dynamic lights into the
active Iris shaderpack pipeline on NeoForge.

Veil effects normally render outside a shaderpack's G-buffer and may disappear,
use incorrect depth information, or fail to contribute light while Iris shaders
are enabled. This mod translates Veil shader programs for Iris and provides a
dedicated shaderpack-compatible pass for Veil point and area lights.

## Features

- Renders Veil shader effects through the active Iris shaderpack pipeline.
- Injects Veil vertex and fragment behavior into compatible Iris G-buffer programs.
- Preserves shaderpack depth, albedo, fog, and render-target behavior where possible.
- Supports opaque and translucent Veil effects.
- Uses Iris `EntitiesTrans` programs for translucent effects when available.
- Provides screen-door dithering as a fallback for shaderpacks without a suitable translucent program.
- Supports Veil effects in Iris shadow-map rendering.
- Automatically rebuilds and caches translated shaders after shaderpack reloads.
- Adds shaderpack-compatible Veil point lights.
- Adds shaderpack-compatible Veil area and cone lights.
- Supports multiple differently colored lights and additive color mixing.
- Applies perceptual color normalization so red, green, and blue lights have more consistent perceived brightness.
- Reconstructs world positions from the shaderpack depth buffer.
- Reconstructs surface normals for angle-dependent diffuse lighting.
- Supports distance attenuation and area-light cone attenuation.
- Supports voxel-based light occlusion and block shadows.
- Supports solid blocks and partially transmissive leaves as voxel shadow casters.
- Uses tone-mapped light contribution to preserve surface texture under bright or overlapping lights.
- Includes an in-game light-rendering configuration screen.
- Includes Performance, Balanced, and High rendering-quality presets.
- Configuration can be opened with `O` by default or from the mod list.
- Exposure, color strength, neutral brightness, and color luminance normalization can be adjusted live.
- Color saturation can be adjusted independently from brightness and exposure.
- Exposure can be adjusted up to 2.00 and color strength up to 12.00.
- Adds live Compat pass diagnostics to the F3 debug screen.
- Configuration is saved to `config/veil_iris_lights.json`.
- Automatically supports standard Veil point and area lights created by other mods.
- Provides tag and Java API hooks for non-occluding light-source blocks.
- Keeps non-shaderpack world lighting on the normal Veil/YesMenn path.

## Requirements

- Minecraft 1.21.1
- NeoForge 21.1.228 or newer
- Iris 1.8.14 beta 1 or newer
- Veil 4.1.4 or newer
- Sodium 0.8.12 beta 1 or newer

## Compatibility Notes

- Shaderpacks differ significantly internally. The mod uses Iris G-buffer
  programs and common render targets, but unusual shaderpack layouts may still
  require compatibility work.
- Voxel shadows use a camera-centered 64 x 64 x 64 block grid.
- Voxel shadows are block based. Complex partial models are approximated, while
  leaves use partial transmission rather than texture-alpha sampling.
- The shaderpack light pass supports configurable budgets of up to 96 point
  lights and 32 area lights per frame.
- This is a client-side rendering compatibility mod. It does not add light
  blocks or other gameplay content by itself.

